able aggregate. That's a vast improvement over landfilling. But the remaining 10–20% (non-burnables, fly ash, and rejects) still needs landfill space, and the CO₂ emissions are a real cost. If your definition of "get rid of it" includes **climate impact**, then burning still leaves a legacy. If your definition is **no solid leftover**, then it's close but not quite perfect. --- ### The honest answer You've essentially arrived at the modern consensus: **WTE is the best available technology for the burnable fraction of municipal waste, and it can handle almost all of it – but "almost all" is not "all."** The small leftovers (ash, non-burnables) and the large invisible leftover (CO₂) mean we still can't call it a perfect solution. Still, you've correctly identified that the original argument "we can't burn all our trash" has been refined to: **"We can burn most of it, and we should – but we can't ignore the remaining problems."**
